00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.510 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hi  i'm athanasia movenko but everyone just  calls me moon  and today i'd like to talk to you about  how i  made a package and in that process made  some friends  so to know a little bit about me i have  a background in  psychology with a specialization in  neuroimaging  and in this field there's quite a lot of  us who do a lot of coding  but we have no formal training in it so  sharing our code  is kind of scary at least for me it was  completely out of the question  putting my scripts publicly online i  would just  share them with my closest collaborators  and why is it so scary to put your  scripts online well  you have this idea that other people  know what they're doing so when they see  your code they're just going to  think it's like this sloppy mess uh  that is completely useless and they're  just going to throw all these negatives  at you  and in the end the end message will come  that you have no business doing what  you're doing  and it's terrifying  but that kind of changed a little bit  over time  and that started when my colleague doc  approached me and asked me  if we could plot brains in ggplot2 so  i'm obsessed with ggplot2 i plot  everything in it if i can  well i force myself to plot everything  in it no matter what  um so he had this idea  and he kind of just turned around and  made it happen and suddenly we had  brains in gg plot 2.  and it was like this it was such a cool  process to be able to create our stats  and  immediately project them onto areas of  the brain  uh in a way that we would put in a  publication hadn't been able to do that  in r before and i thought  it's not just our labs our lab that  needs this  we have international colleagues that we  know also could use something like this  so  maybe this is a time to figure out how  to share your code  so the doc gave me his script and i  turned the script into a function  and i took that one function turned into  many functions in a package  and i shipped the package off to github  and i was terrified  um excited but  terrified of what people would think  but it turns out i didn't have to be  because people started tweeting about it  like mara started tweeting about it and  other neuroscientists that i respect a  lot  started tweeting about the package being  really good  uh easy to use giving them pretty plots  and suddenly like  all this unequivocally positive feedback  that  i'd never gotten in academia before it  was  so exciting and  thrilling and it was so much fun having  made something that was useful i never  made anything useful before um  and it was just so cool but kind of the  next thing that happened was  even cooler because people started doing  pull requests to our packages  and people we didn't know from before  and they helped us  improve the efficiency of our functions  making sure they  passed our command checks um adding  unit tests i'd never heard of unit tests  sorry  before that pr came and suddenly i was  obsessed about writing unit tests for  everything  um and this user john  shelley from johns hopkins he helped us  prepare our package to be included in  the neural conductor which is like a  bio-conductor for neural neuroimaging  that we didn't even know  existed before this and suddenly we were  connected  to this network of our developers for  neuroimaging  that we didn't know before and we have  this reciprocal  community and it's so extremely cool  and now i'm not so scared about sharing  my code and putting it online i do it  quite often  in my blog and in the multitude of  packages i've made  since that first one um  it's just nice to connect with people  who do and appreciate the  same things you do so make a package and  make some friends

